const specs=[
  {
    "id": "viz-comparison",
    "comps": [
      "m1.c1"
    ],
    "title": "Compare categories with aligned positions and bars",
    "intro": "A branch manager needs to compare support workload across teams. The chart should make differences easy to judge without requiring readers to decode area, perspective or decorative effects.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Choose the visual reasoning",
        "For a small number of categories, bars provide aligned lengths that support magnitude comparison. A dot plot can use aligned positions when comparing values around a meaningful range. Choose horizontal bars when category labels are long, and use a justified category order such as magnitude or a meaningful business sequence.",
        "Counts and rates answer different questions. A workload count describes total demand; a resolution rate describes successes per eligible case. Label the measure and units rather than letting a chart type imply a denominator. Avoid adding irrelevant categories or unrelated series merely because the plotting library accepts them."
      ],
      [
        "Inspect the constructed chart",
        "The fixture uses horizontal bars for three synthetic teams, labels the workload unit and sorts the values explicitly. Inspecting artist widths verifies that the plotted magnitudes match the specified counts. A zero baseline is deliberately set because bar length is the chosen magnitude encoding.",
        "A bar chart is not always preferable to a table: exact lookup, many narrow categories or detailed accounting may require a compact table instead. If a message concerns change from a prior value, a paired dot or slope chart may show that difference more directly than two crowded sets of bars."
      ]
    ],
    "lang": "python-pandas",
    "problem": "Compare categories with aligned positions and bars on a synthetic plot.",
    "steps": [
      "State the reader\u2019s comparison and the units.",
      "Construct the relevant Matplotlib artists and inspect their properties.",
      "Render a small PNG in memory and inspect its file signature."
    ],
    "code": "import os\nos.environ[\"MPLCONFIGDIR\"]=\"/tmp/mpl\"\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom io import BytesIO\nf,a=plt.subplots(figsize=(3,2))\na.barh([\"C\",\"A\",\"B\"],[5,10,20])\na.set_xlabel(\"Eligible tickets\")\na.set_xlim(left=0)\nprint([int(p.get_width()) for p in a.patches])\nprint(a.get_xlabel(),int(a.get_xlim()[0]))\nb=BytesIO()\nf.savefig(b,format=\"png\",dpi=60)\nprint(b.getvalue()[:8]==b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\")\nplt.close(f)",
    "expect": "[5, 10, 20]\nEligible tickets 0\nTrue",
