    "title": "Show time trends without inventing continuity",
    "intro": "A revenue report needs to show changes over dates. Connecting points implies an ordered relationship; missing periods, uneven intervals and changing observation windows can alter what the line appears to say.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Choose the visual reasoning",
        "Use a line chart when the horizontal coordinate has meaningful order, often time, and connecting observations aids interpretation. Sort by the actual time values rather than source row position. Show meaningful date intervals and distinguish an irregular event series from regular period aggregates.",
        "An absent value is not the same as zero. A gap in collection should usually remain visible rather than become a fabricated drop to zero or an unjustified interpolated bridge. State whether values are daily totals, moving averages or cumulative measures, because each changes the meaning of the trend."
      ],
      [
        "Inspect the constructed chart",
        "The fixture plots four ordered positions with one unavailable observation using NaN. Matplotlib retains the missing value in the line data and breaks the rendered line there. The assertion checks missing support rather than treating the empty period as evidence of inactivity.",
        "Compare equivalent windows and identify partial periods. A seasonal cycle, expanded reporting population or changed definition can explain a visible slope without an intervention effect. A small multiple with shared scales can make regional trends easier to compare than an overcrowded multi-line chart."
