    "id": "viz-aggregation",
    "comps": [
      "m2.c1"
    ],
    "title": "Expose denominators, aggregation and uncertainty in charts",
    "intro": "A regional chart ranks success percentages without showing how many cases support them. Honest visualization includes the analytical choices that determine each mark, not only the polished final values.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Choose the visual reasoning",
        "State the observation grain, inclusion rule and denominator. A stacked bar may show counts, shares within each group or composition across all observations; these are distinct measures. A 100% stacked bar normalizes each total and hides differences in group volume unless support is reported separately.",
        "Aggregated values can conceal subgroup differences and composition shifts. A mean alone can hide tails; an overall rate can reverse within meaningful strata. Choose an appropriate disaggregation when it changes the question, and explain any standardization rather than presenting adjusted values as raw observations."
      ],
      [
        "Inspect the constructed chart",
        "The fixture calculates success rates from explicit numerators and denominators and places both rates in a bar chart. Labels include eligible counts. The code checks the exact plotted proportions and labels; it supplies no sampling model or uncertainty interval.",
        "Error bars require a defined meaning: standard deviation, standard error, confidence interval, prediction interval and range are not interchangeable. Name the measure, calculation and assumptions. Do not invent precision from a tiny convenience sample or use decorative error bars without an underlying estimate."
      ]
    ],
    "lang": "python-pandas",
    "problem": "Expose denominators, aggregation and uncertainty in charts on a synthetic plot.",
    "steps": [
      "State the reader\u2019s comparison and the units.",
      "Construct the relevant Matplotlib artists and inspect their properties.",
      "Render a small PNG in memory and inspect its file signature."
    ],
    "code": "import os\nos.environ[\"MPLCONFIGDIR\"]=\"/tmp/mpl\"\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom io import BytesIO\nwins=[1,80]\ntotals=[2,100]\nrates=[w/n for w,n in zip(wins,totals)]\nf,a=plt.subplots(figsize=(3,2))\na.bar([\"A (n=2)\",\"B (n=100)\"],rates)\na.set_ylim(0,1)\na.set_ylabel(\"Observed success proportion\")\nprint([round(p.get_height(),2) for p in a.patches])\nprint([t.get_text() for t in a.get_xticklabels()])\nb=BytesIO()\nf.savefig(b,format=\"png\",dpi=60)\nprint(b.getvalue()[:8]==b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\")\nplt.close(f)",
    "expect": "[0.5, 0.8]\n['A (n=2)', 'B (n=100)']\nTrue",
    "limit": "Matplotlib 3.10.8 uses the noninteractive Agg backend. Assertions inspect chart data, settings and a real in-memory PNG signature under the existing 2 CPU-second/1 GiB sandbox. They do not establish visual legibility, assistive-technology compatibility, correct business interpretation or human design approval.",
    "mistakes": [
      "A successfully rendered image can still mislead if its scope or encoding is inappropriate.",
