    "title": "Use color with redundant cues and appropriate palettes",
    "intro": "A status chart uses red and green with no labels. Readers with different color perception, grayscale printouts or poor displays may lose the distinction, so color should support rather than carry all essential meaning.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Choose the visual reasoning",
        "Use qualitative colors for nominal categories, a sequential palette for ordered magnitude, and a diverging palette when a meaningful midpoint separates opposite directions. A diverging color map needs a justified center, not merely a preference for two bright hues. Perceptual lightness can help communicate ordered values more consistently.",
        "Use labels, marker shapes, line styles or patterns as redundant cues for essential distinctions. Avoid relying on red-versus-green alone. Check the actual output at its intended size and in relevant viewing conditions; a palette name does not certify accessibility, contrast or success for every reader."
      ],
      [
        "Inspect the constructed chart",
        "The fixture constructs two lines with both different line styles and marker shapes, labels each series and renders an image. Assertions inspect the redundant style choices and legend text. They verify construction, not whether a person can perceive every mark or whether a screen reader receives an equivalent explanation.",
        "Reserve emphasis colors for the intended message and keep supporting context restrained. Too many equally saturated categories compete for attention. Direct labels can reduce dependence on a distant legend, while a textual summary and data table can provide another route to the evidence."
