  {
    "id": "viz-message",
    "comps": [
      "m3.c1"
    ],
    "title": "Write titles and annotations that guide without overstating",
    "intro": "A chart can be technically correct yet leave readers searching for the point. Titles, labels and annotations should explain the intended observation while preserving the boundaries of the evidence.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Choose the visual reasoning",
        "Use a title that states a scoped, defensible message or a clear analytical question. Add a subtitle or caption for the population, period and definition. Avoid causal verbs such as caused or fixed when the evidence is only descriptive, and distinguish a target line from an observed result.",
        "Place annotations near the relevant marks and use concise text. A note should identify what happened or why a point deserves attention without hiding neighboring data. Direct labels can reduce legend lookup, but dense text may require selective labeling or a separate table."
      ],
      [
        "Inspect the constructed chart",
        "The fixture labels an observed series, draws a clearly named target line and annotates one point. The assertions verify the actual title, annotation count and target label. The render does not validate the narrative: a reviewer must still assess whether the wording follows from the data and whether labels overlap.",
        "Let the reader distinguish observation, interpretation and recommendation. A meaningful highlight can focus the question, while a caption records uncertainty and caveats. Supporting details should remain available without forcing every data point to carry a long paragraph in the plot area."
      ]
    ],
    "lang": "python-pandas",
    "problem": "Write titles and annotations that guide without overstating on a synthetic plot.",
    "steps": [
